1
00:00:01,566 --> 00:00:04,200
You walk into a phone shop just to replace the battery

2
00:00:04,266 --> 00:00:06,400
The clerk glances at your old phone

3
00:00:06,566 --> 00:00:08,900
He says this one really isn't worth fixing

4
00:00:08,900 --> 00:00:11,566
Add two thousand more and you can get a better one

5
00:00:11,800 --> 00:00:13,033
He sounds sincere

6
00:00:13,033 --> 00:00:15,166
Not a single spec he cites is a lie

7
00:00:15,300 --> 00:00:17,800
You even feel he's trying to save you money

8
00:00:18,200 --> 00:00:21,500
But this is exactly the moment Munger most wants to warn you about

9
00:00:21,800 --> 00:00:24,066
The most dangerous person isn't necessarily a scammer

10
00:00:24,100 --> 00:00:27,566
It's someone who truly believes something that happens to benefit himself

11
00:00:28,100 --> 00:00:30,266
Because a scammer knows he's lying

12
00:00:30,266 --> 00:00:32,133
So you stay at least a little on guard

13
00:00:32,366 --> 00:00:35,600
But when someone gains from a particular answer

14
00:00:35,633 --> 00:00:38,800
His brain actively hunts for evidence to support it

15
00:00:38,966 --> 00:00:41,333
Hunt long enough and he ends up believing it himself

16
00:00:41,500 --> 00:00:45,166
That clerk may genuinely feel the new phone suits you better

17
00:00:45,400 --> 00:00:48,933
The financial advisor may genuinely think that product is good

18
00:00:49,200 --> 00:00:54,100
The boss may genuinely believe one more metric will boost efficiency

19
00:00:54,233 --> 00:00:55,500
They aren't necessarily bad people

20
00:00:55,600 --> 00:00:59,366
But the seat they sit in selects which facts they can see

21
00:01:00,100 --> 00:01:02,700
This is the calculation most people get wrong

22
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:05,566
We keep judging whether someone is honest

23
00:01:05,700 --> 00:01:10,900
Yet rarely check which answer their pay, KPI and pride are tied to

24
00:01:11,200 --> 00:01:15,300
At month end you instinctively scan the bonus column on your payslip

25
00:01:15,466 --> 00:01:16,766
Everyone else does too

26
00:01:17,066 --> 00:01:19,100
Money isn't the only incentive

27
00:01:19,433 --> 00:01:26,066
Commission, KPI, likes, promotion, fame, even dodging one scolding are all incentives

28
00:01:26,366 --> 00:01:28,666
They act like an invisible magnet

29
00:01:28,766 --> 00:01:30,566
They don't directly turn people bad

30
00:01:30,700 --> 00:01:34,166
But they slowly pull everyone's attention toward the same direction

31
00:01:34,300 --> 00:01:37,400
So this video isn't teaching you to doubt everyone

32
00:01:37,633 --> 00:01:39,733
It just wants to help you see one layer deeper

33
00:01:40,033 --> 00:01:42,900
Next time someone hands you a very certain answer

34
00:01:43,000 --> 00:01:45,666
Don't rush to judge whether it makes sense

35
00:01:45,700 --> 00:01:46,766
Ask first

36
00:01:46,800 --> 00:01:49,966
If I believe this, who gains

37
00:01:50,666 --> 00:01:55,166
Munger calls this incentive-caused bias

38
00:01:55,366 --> 00:01:57,400
The name sounds stiff

39
00:01:57,666 --> 00:01:59,266
In plain words

40
00:01:59,300 --> 00:02:03,500
Whatever rewards you gets better and better at being seen by you

41
00:02:03,666 --> 00:02:06,266
Munger once told a FedEx story

42
00:02:06,400 --> 00:02:09,700
Night shift workers had to load planes fast

43
00:02:09,766 --> 00:02:13,933
But process, discipline and duty talks did nothing

44
00:02:14,366 --> 00:02:16,700
Then management changed how they paid

45
00:02:16,800 --> 00:02:19,333
No longer just by hours spent

46
00:02:19,566 --> 00:02:23,000
But letting anyone who finished the shift go home early

47
00:02:23,266 --> 00:02:26,366
The same crew cleared the cargo in no time

48
00:02:26,600 --> 00:02:28,700
People didn't suddenly get hardworking

49
00:02:28,766 --> 00:02:31,066
The work didn't suddenly get easier

50
00:02:31,166 --> 00:02:35,333
The reward direction finally lined up with what the company wanted

51
00:02:35,700 --> 00:02:40,500
The real lesson isn't how smart per-shift pay is

52
00:02:40,633 --> 00:02:43,266
It's that persuasion is usually weaker than incentive

53
00:02:43,600 --> 00:02:45,233
Ten slogans on the wall

54
00:02:45,233 --> 00:02:47,966
Can lose to one number on the paysheet

55
00:02:48,166 --> 00:02:50,866
You've probably seen this at your company

56
00:02:51,100 --> 00:02:53,600
When support is graded on call count

57
00:02:53,700 --> 00:02:55,466
Calls get shorter and shorter

58
00:02:55,800 --> 00:02:58,066
When sales is graded on deal size

59
00:02:58,300 --> 00:03:01,533
Bad-fit clients get pitched as perfect matches

60
00:03:01,866 --> 00:03:04,000
When a content team is graded on click rate

61
00:03:04,166 --> 00:03:06,333
Headlines get more and more provocative

62
00:03:06,466 --> 00:03:09,566
These people aren't scheming to deceive every morning

63
00:03:09,766 --> 00:03:12,733
More often they hit the target first

64
00:03:12,866 --> 00:03:16,666
Then let the brain backfill a plausible explanation

65
00:03:16,866 --> 00:03:18,366
The client really does need it

66
00:03:18,433 --> 00:03:19,933
Users really do love it

67
00:03:20,433 --> 00:03:22,533
The team really should push harder

68
00:03:22,800 --> 00:03:25,966
The more they explain, the deeper they believe

69
00:03:26,233 --> 00:03:28,166
That's where the problem is

70
00:03:28,466 --> 00:03:31,733
Once a person is rewarded by a conclusion

71
00:03:31,900 --> 00:03:33,800
He doesn't just defend the interest

72
00:03:33,900 --> 00:03:37,266
He also defends the story that makes him feel justified

73
00:03:37,500 --> 00:03:42,066
So incentive bias is often harder to spot than outright lying

74
00:03:42,266 --> 00:03:44,366
Outright lying leaves traces

75
00:03:44,466 --> 00:03:49,500
Sincere self-persuasion can look complete in face, tone and evidence

76
00:03:49,766 --> 00:03:52,566
You walk away thinking this person is so professional

77
00:03:52,600 --> 00:03:55,200
But professional only means he can explain

78
00:03:55,433 --> 00:03:58,200
Not that his viewing angle isn't tilted

79
00:03:58,566 --> 00:04:00,800
Interest doesn't automatically invalidate a view

80
00:04:00,900 --> 00:04:03,366
But interest is always worth seeing

81
00:04:04,300 --> 00:04:06,666
Start with the most common case, sales recommendations

82
00:04:06,833 --> 00:04:08,000
You go get glasses

83
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:10,533
The clerk says these lenses are better for your eyes

84
00:04:10,666 --> 00:04:12,300
Maybe they really are better

85
00:04:12,433 --> 00:04:15,400
But if different lenses pay him different commissions

86
00:04:15,500 --> 00:04:18,900
That fact should sit on the table next to the specs

87
00:04:19,033 --> 00:04:21,166
Not to prove he's lying

88
00:04:21,466 --> 00:04:25,900
But to remind you he more easily sees the merits of the pricier pair

89
00:04:26,066 --> 00:04:27,366
Now take financial advisors

90
00:04:27,433 --> 00:04:29,266
Some products suit you

91
00:04:29,366 --> 00:04:32,733
Some products suit his sales quota better

92
00:04:32,900 --> 00:04:34,600
The two sometimes overlap

93
00:04:34,800 --> 00:04:37,466
But you can't assume they always do

94
00:04:37,700 --> 00:04:41,066
When you hear "capital-guaranteed, high return, limited spots"

95
00:04:41,200 --> 00:04:42,900
Don't get carried away by the tone

96
00:04:43,066 --> 00:04:46,700
First check whether his pay comes from fees or commissions

97
00:04:46,900 --> 00:04:50,000
How he's paid won't tell you if the answer is right

98
00:04:50,200 --> 00:04:54,000
But it tells you which answer is easier for him to give

99
00:04:54,366 --> 00:04:56,366
KPIs inside a company work the same way

100
00:04:56,666 --> 00:04:59,166
If a department only watches new users

101
00:04:59,400 --> 00:05:03,133
It tends to ignore whether existing users are happy

102
00:05:03,366 --> 00:05:05,866
If a team only watches launch speed

103
00:05:05,966 --> 00:05:09,933
Testing and maintenance become someone else's future headache

104
00:05:10,166 --> 00:05:13,900
The arguments you see in weekly meetings look like disagreement

105
00:05:14,200 --> 00:05:17,733
Underneath, everyone may just be carrying a different scoreboard

106
00:05:18,200 --> 00:05:19,666
One guards revenue

107
00:05:19,666 --> 00:05:21,100
One guards cost

108
00:05:21,100 --> 00:05:22,600
One guards schedule

109
00:05:22,800 --> 00:05:24,900
They all talk about the company's interest

110
00:05:25,066 --> 00:05:29,166
Yet each one's company happens to look like his own scorecard

111
00:05:29,633 --> 00:05:31,300
Knowledge creators aren't exempt either

112
00:05:31,466 --> 00:05:35,466
Course makers more easily believe the problem needs a course

113
00:05:35,666 --> 00:05:39,600
Tool sellers more easily believe the problem needs a tool

114
00:05:39,833 --> 00:05:44,200
Book-summary creators more easily sell one book as a master key

115
00:05:44,366 --> 00:05:45,900
Including me talking about Munger right now

116
00:05:45,900 --> 00:05:50,100
You should also ask what this content hopes to get from you

117
00:05:50,100 --> 00:05:51,266
Watch time

118
00:05:51,266 --> 00:05:52,033
Saves

119
00:05:52,033 --> 00:05:53,066
Or follows

120
00:05:53,433 --> 00:05:56,333
Laying this out won't destroy trust

121
00:05:56,400 --> 00:06:00,300
Trust that truly lasts was never afraid of seeing the interest

122
00:06:00,566 --> 00:06:02,400
Platform recommendations are even more direct

123
00:06:02,866 --> 00:06:06,100
You think the home feed helps you find what matters most

124
00:06:06,200 --> 00:06:10,466
But what the platform first needs is usually to keep you scrolling

125
00:06:10,700 --> 00:06:14,266
Most important and hardest to close are not the same standard

126
00:06:14,600 --> 00:06:18,400
Trending lists make something look like everyone cares

127
00:06:18,633 --> 00:06:22,100
Yet the algorithm may not be answering what's worth your attention

128
00:06:22,266 --> 00:06:24,666
It's just completing its own reward

129
00:06:24,833 --> 00:06:26,466
Finally, AI answers

130
00:06:26,566 --> 00:06:29,733
AI has no payslip and takes no commission

131
00:06:29,966 --> 00:06:32,766
But it's still shaped by goals and feedback

132
00:06:32,966 --> 00:06:37,200
The system wants it helpful, human and satisfying

133
00:06:37,666 --> 00:06:42,900
So when a question is vague, it may lean toward a complete smooth answer

134
00:06:43,066 --> 00:06:44,766
Complete doesn't mean correct

135
00:06:44,866 --> 00:06:47,566
Comfortable doesn't mean verifiable

136
00:06:47,766 --> 00:06:50,400
If you only reward it for answering fast

137
00:06:50,600 --> 00:06:54,400
It rarely stops to ask what you're really trying to solve

138
00:06:54,633 --> 00:06:57,133
So don't think of incentives as only money

139
00:06:57,366 --> 00:07:00,966
Anything repeatedly rewarded slowly reshapes the answer

140
00:07:02,066 --> 00:07:03,933
So what should ordinary people do

141
00:07:04,200 --> 00:07:08,600
Doubt everyone who speaks, assuming they have a hidden motive

142
00:07:08,733 --> 00:07:09,533
No

143
00:07:09,900 --> 00:07:13,633
If you only learn to doubt, you don't gain judgment

144
00:07:13,633 --> 00:07:14,600
Just exhaustion

145
00:07:15,000 --> 00:07:19,733
The useful thing about Munger's reminder is it doesn't ask you to read minds

146
00:07:19,833 --> 00:07:21,200
You only need to read structure

147
00:07:21,700 --> 00:07:25,933
Next time someone urges you to buy, invest or back a plan

148
00:07:26,033 --> 00:07:28,700
Set aside their point for ten seconds

149
00:07:28,866 --> 00:07:29,700
And ask

150
00:07:30,066 --> 00:07:33,700
If I believe this, who gains

151
00:07:33,966 --> 00:07:35,366
The gain can be money

152
00:07:35,400 --> 00:07:39,966
Or performance, status, traffic, blame avoidance and convenience

153
00:07:40,266 --> 00:07:43,366
If the answer is they gain directly

154
00:07:43,566 --> 00:07:45,100
Don't dismiss them right away

155
00:07:45,466 --> 00:07:49,400
Just switch trust from automatic to manual review

156
00:07:49,666 --> 00:07:52,000
Ask one more person who has no stake in it

157
00:07:52,600 --> 00:07:55,166
Read material that doesn't profit from the deal

158
00:07:55,466 --> 00:07:58,400
Find the evidence most unfavorable to this conclusion

159
00:07:58,500 --> 00:08:02,066
Then see if they're willing to spell out the risks too

160
00:08:02,233 --> 00:08:06,133
A truly reliable professional doesn't pretend to have no interests

161
00:08:06,366 --> 00:08:09,266
He tells you upfront how he charges

162
00:08:09,366 --> 00:08:11,100
Which cases don't fit

163
00:08:11,166 --> 00:08:13,033
What outcomes he can't guarantee

164
00:08:13,033 --> 00:08:17,266
Most importantly, whether he shares the same downside as you

165
00:08:17,633 --> 00:08:20,000
If the recommendation fails, do only you pay

166
00:08:20,066 --> 00:08:22,800
Or does he also lose pay and reputation

167
00:08:23,166 --> 00:08:26,366
Aligned interests don't guarantee a correct judgment

168
00:08:26,500 --> 00:08:30,366
But misaligned interests are reason enough not to hand over the wheel

169
00:08:30,633 --> 00:08:32,466
Here's another practical method

170
00:08:32,600 --> 00:08:35,733
After hearing a recommendation, try swapping their reward

171
00:08:35,866 --> 00:08:39,900
If the salesperson weren't paid on the deal, would he still recommend the same one

172
00:08:40,200 --> 00:08:44,766
If the department weren't measured on this metric, would it still push the same plan

173
00:08:44,833 --> 00:08:50,366
If this content had no likes or shares, would it still sound this certain

174
00:08:50,633 --> 00:08:53,366
This question may not yield a real answer

175
00:08:53,500 --> 00:08:56,933
But it pulls you out of the script and back to the relationship map

176
00:08:57,366 --> 00:08:58,533
Opinions are like water

177
00:08:58,566 --> 00:09:00,133
Incentives are more like the riverbed

178
00:09:00,300 --> 00:09:04,133
Stare only at the surface and every sentence looks free-flowing

179
00:09:04,266 --> 00:09:08,400
Map the riverbed and you see why it always runs that way

180
00:09:08,666 --> 00:09:11,900
Ordinary people can't fact-check every sentence in life

181
00:09:12,100 --> 00:09:18,000
But on big-money, high-stakes, hard-to-undo decisions, a glance at the riverbed pays off

182
00:09:18,366 --> 00:09:21,266
You can also turn this question on yourself

183
00:09:21,633 --> 00:09:24,266
When you badly want to believe an answer

184
00:09:24,366 --> 00:09:26,566
Ask what it gives you

185
00:09:26,666 --> 00:09:28,833
The convenience of skipping more research

186
00:09:28,833 --> 00:09:30,266
Saving face

187
00:09:30,300 --> 00:09:33,100
Or making a past choice look not wrong

188
00:09:33,433 --> 00:09:36,700
The incentive hardest to spot usually isn't someone else's

189
00:09:36,833 --> 00:09:39,966
It's that bit of comfort in our own hearts we won't admit

190
00:09:40,200 --> 00:09:42,100
Back to the phone shop at the start

191
00:09:42,233 --> 00:09:44,800
The clerk pushes the new phone toward you

192
00:09:44,800 --> 00:09:46,133
You don't need to accuse him

193
00:09:46,200 --> 00:09:49,333
Or rush to prove the old phone still has five good years

194
00:09:49,566 --> 00:09:54,000
Just clarify the repair price, the upgrade commission and your real needs

195
00:09:54,266 --> 00:09:56,800
Then take the decision back into your own hands

196
00:09:57,000 --> 00:09:59,533
Listening to what someone says is hearing the answer

197
00:09:59,966 --> 00:10:04,733
Watching what they're rewarded for is seeing where the answer grows from

198
00:10:05,033 --> 00:10:08,066
If I believe this, who gains

199
00:10:08,233 --> 00:10:10,300
This sentence won't decide for you

200
00:10:10,466 --> 00:10:14,900
But it can quiet down a lot of overly certain voices
